<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Seat harness ~ a capability never lent by ambient authority

A seat holds only the capabilities its wall grants. Every act runs under exactly one authority, and the harness names it. Each lineage (Codex, Copilot, Claude) wears the same seat spec, so a wall keeps one meaning across them. The crew shapes and the lane law stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>>; this carrier holds the machinery beneath them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-trust-model`>>
!! The trust model ~ who holds each capability, whose authority runs each act

|! Actor |! Holds |! Runs under its own authority |! Never |
| ''Operator'' | rulings, credentials, the push of the span and `main`, every founding and custody act | anything | delegates a credential to a seat |
| ''Controller'' | the one go per push, seat assignment, the lane's merge, the read of every chunk | its own commits and pushes | executes code from a lane tree it has not read |
| ''Broker'' (`tools/commit-relay.mjs`) | fixed data acts a lead requests: a commit of named paths, a push the controller's go names, a builder worktree, a walled seat's launch | git and the launcher, from a path outside the lane | grants a go; launches a lead; touches a path outside the lane root |
| ''Lead'' | its worktree and its lane root, no git dir and no network flag (the model API rides the codex process, outside the sandbox) | edits, tests, briefs, requests to the broker | commits or pushes itself; writes the main checkout |
| ''Builder'' | its worktree alone | edits, typecheck, single-file tests | commits; reaches the network |
| ''Reader'' | nothing writable | reads | writes any file; its final message carries the hand-back |

''The trust boundary of the broker.'' The broker executes no code from a lead's tree. It runs data-only git plumbing (`write-tree`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref` with an expected old head) through a pinned absolute `git`, a fixed environment (attributes read from the empty tree), an isolated home and temp dir, and a push that runs from a throwaway repo of the broker's own to the controller's remote URL, so the lane's `.git/config` (pushurl, insteadOf, helpers) is never read, so a planted hook, credential helper, `.gitattributes` filter, submodule, poisoned `PATH`, `GIT_*` or `NODE_OPTIONS` reaches nothing. Hooks and validators run in a separate ''check seat'' behind bubblewrap (no network, a read-only root, one writable work dir) over an exact export of the tree; the broker commits only when it verifies that seat's signed receipt, which covers the tree, the check names and the check definitions; the checked tree is bound read-only for every check, and the key and the receipts live in the broker's memory alone, never on disk, because a workspace-write sandbox fences writes, not reads, and it refuses a hardlinked file. Installs run in an ''install seat'' that binds the worktree, the store, the git dir and every protected path by bubblewrap's fd binds (`--bind-fd`, `--ro-bind-fd`) over directories held open once, and reads `.npmrc` through the held worktree handle with O_NOFOLLOW (a symlink there refuses by name before any read) (scripts off, pnpmfile ignored, offline, a refused `.npmrc` key by name, `.git` read-only, the controller dir read as empty so the key and the go stay unreadable). The proofs live in `tools/commit-relay.test.mjs`. The request directory is held open by handle (`/proc/self/fd` anchoring) so a swapped `relay` redirects nothing. A new worktree is built where it stays, in a broker-owned staging directory, and the lane shows it as a symlink the broker makes through a parent directory it holds open, with rollback through the same handle. The trusted launcher must sit outside the scratch tree entirely, and the broker's own state dir is checked by real path: outside the lane, every worktree and the whole scratch lane tree, before ANY directory (relay/ included) is created. Two edges remain open: the receipt key lives in the broker process's memory, so a same-user `ptrace` of the broker would read it (the host's `ptrace_scope` and the seat's seccomp are the wall there), and the wall rests on `bwrap` being present (its absence refuses by name, never runs unwalled).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seat-spec`>>
!! The seat spec ~ one shape, rendered per lineage

A seat names its lineage, model, effort, mode, wall, seat directory under `~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/`, and its brief file. `tools/launch-codex.mjs` renders it into Codex flags and `tools/launch-copilot.mjs` into Copilot tool gates.

|! Mode |! Writes |! Network |! Commits |
| `read` | nothing (the final message carries the hand-back) | off | never |
| `research` | its seat directory | on, with search | never |
| `build` | its worktree and seat directory | off | never; its lead lands the diff |
| `lead` (Codex) | its worktree, the repo's git dir, its lane root | on | through the broker alone |

''Walls that hold by measure.'' The Codex sandbox keeps `.git` read-only inside every granted root, so no sandboxed Codex seat commits. A seat launched inside another seat's sandbox inherits that sandbox and cannot initialize its own runtime state, so the broker launches every seat a lead asks for. A Copilot build seat's tool gate may deny `node` and `pnpm`; its lead or the controller then runs the tests.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lead`>>
!! The lead ~ fresh per step, never one long session

''The measured controller recipe'' (2026-11-06: G54 #177 and slot-wake #178 both landed). Provision with `node tools/provision-worktree.mjs <lane-root>/wt`, switch at its base with `git switch -c <branch>`, and run `pnpm build`. Write `<lane-root>/state.json` with `head`, `owed`, `seats`, `worktrees`, `next`, `step` and `trailers` (the session's attribution lines, set once; the brief carries them to every lead step); validate with `tools/lane-state.mjs` `readState`. Write a `--checks` JSON with a structural no-conflict-markers check, then start `node tools/commit-relay.mjs --lane-root … --worktree … --branch … --launcher <main-tree/tools/launch-codex.mjs> --checks … --remote-url …` in the harness's background mode. The install door also takes `--installer <the real pnpm, with node beside it> --store-dir <the pnpm store> --cache-dir <the pnpm metadata cache>`; the relay refuses an install by naming each flag it lacks, and a corepack shim cannot run inside the install wall.

Launch each step with `node tools/launch-codex.mjs --lead-step --lane-root … --model gpt-6.1-terra --effort medium --worktree …`. Between steps the controller reads `relay/res-NNN.json`, trues `head`, and writes `next` with the house subject `lar:///ha.ka.ba/<pkg>/<area>/<w1.w2.w3> — summary`. The lead has no git or network: the controller runs `node tools/pre-push-gate.mjs` and the lane's tests outside the wall (empty stderr inside the wall came from that wall), then `node tools/push-go.mjs --lane-root … --branch … --head … --remote-url …`; the next lead step requests the push through the broker. The controller opens the PR, writes the land message before the wait, reads CI, writes a SHIP for a security-class lane, lands, stops the broker and removes the worktree.

* `tools/lane-state.mjs` holds the shape and `node tools/launch-codex.mjs --lead-step --lane-root <dir> --model <m> --effort <e> --worktree <wt>` launches the step: it reads `<lane-root>/state.json`, writes `<lane-root>/steps/step-NNN/brief.md` from the state, and launches a lead there with the lane root added so the lead can rewrite the state. The step counter spends only when the preflight passes, a missing or misshapen state refuses by name, and the lead ceiling bounds the step.
* A lead runs ONE step per launch: it reads its lane's state file (head, owed rows, live seats, worktree map), does that step, writes the state file and its hand-back, and stops. The next step launches a fresh lead over the new state.
* A step stays short: about 10 to 15 minutes or 40 tool calls. An agentic session re-sends its whole transcript on every call, so one long session grows its draw roughly with the square of its length.
* A lead dispatches and waits outside the model: the broker or the controller's watcher blocks on a seat, and the next lead step reads the result path. A lead never polls inside its own context; its own broker receipt may use the bounded file-event wait under #/the-broker.
* A failing test counts as work. A lead stops only at a wall, at a design question that needs an operator ruling (named with its options), or at the push go.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-broker`>>
!! The broker ~ the hands outside a walled lead

* ''Checks at the wall'' (measured 2026-11-06, G54): the broker refuses every commit without `--checks` ("no checks configured for the check seat"). The check seat's bwrap wall binds `/usr` alone, while Node lives under `~/.nvm`, so no house Node gate runs inside it. Until the owed toolchain binding lands, the controller runs the gate outside the wall in the lane worktree and the broker checks structurally for conflict markers.

* ''The swap'': `tools/seat-broker.mjs` holds the plumbing doors, `tools/seat-walls.mjs` the check and install seats, `tools/tree-export.mjs` the filter-free tree export. ''Requests'' arrive as `req-<id>.json` files in `<lane-root>/relay/`, zero-padded and in order, one at a time; each answer lands as `res-<id>.json` before the next request runs. If a lane plants the answer during the act, the broker preserves that file, reports `receipt-clash` in its own stderr and continues; lane-writable answers never certify a commit. A lead may read its own `res-<id>.json` in the same step through `node tools/wait-receipt.mjs <lane-root>/relay/res-<id>.json`, an event wait for the file to appear, bounded by a named `// LIVENESS:` timeout for a broker that died (120 seconds by default, capped in code at 300 seconds; expiry names `receipt-timeout`); no receipt polling and no in-context seat waiting follow from this exception. Unknown kinds and unknown fields refuse by name.
* ''Doors'': `commit` (named relative paths read race-proof from the worktree, the message as inline text (size-capped; the broker opens no lead-controlled file for it), an expected old head, a verified check receipt; no hook, no filter); `push` (only when the go file names the branch AND the head; never `-u`, never force; the go spends on the attempt); `worktree` (a new path under the lane root, a branch carrying the lane prefix, a filter-free export of the base tree, submodule paths left empty); `install` (the install seat over a worktree in the lane root); `worktree-remove` (a registered, clean tree, never the broker's own, never `--force`); `launch` (`build`, `read` or `research` alone, never `lead`; allowlisted models; a seat directory inside the lane root with its brief).
* ''Test door'': `test` runs named files with Node or package-local Vitest in a PID, IPC and network namespace; it binds the worktree writable with `.git` read-only, scratch writable, and the active Node version and system tools read-only. For Vitest, one request names files in one package: the wall runs that package's build and `tools/stamp-build.mjs . --pkg <dir>` before testing; a build or typecheck failure returns `ok:false` with its error lines and never reaches tests. Named files reach Vitest directly without a `--` separator. Thirty seconds without child stdout or stderr stops the group. Tallies advise the requester and never carry a check receipt.
* ''Every door ends'' (`tools/relay-claim.mjs` holds the bounds). One supervisor (`seat-walls` `superviseChild`) runs the test and install children: silence stops the group (thirty seconds for a test, `DOOR_SILENCE_MS` for an install) and so does the whole-run bound `DOOR_RUN_MAX_MS` (30 minutes, above the longest measured local walk of 24), however much the child talks. A check runs synchronously and stops at `DOOR_SILENCE_MS`.
* ''What an install refusal leaves'': the install seat reads the store read-only, so a refused install (pnpm's supply-chain policy check among them) can still leave links in the lane's `node_modules`; they point only into that read-only store, and `ok:false` means the tree's modules read unverified until a clean install. The seat opens the store's index `immutable=1`, so a concurrent store writer outside the wall (the main checkout's install, another provision) can feed it torn reads; that surfaces as an install refusal (it fails closed), and a refusal while a store writer runs reads as that flake first.
* ''The claim'' tells a waiting lead the broker lives. At pickup the relay stands `run-<id>.json` in the relay dir (O_EXCL, so a planted name stays and nothing writes through it), stirs it with `lutimes` at most once per relay pause while the door's child speaks or between checks, and unlinks it after the answer. `tools/wait-receipt.mjs` reads any claim's stir as life: silence past `DOOR_SILENCE_MS` plus two relay pauses reads as a dead broker, and a cap of `DOOR_RUN_MAX_MS` plus two pauses holds whatever the claims do. The lead can write the relay dir, so a claim proves life and never sets a bound; a forged claim that stirs forever ends at the cap.
* ''The go'' lives outside the lane root and the worktree, at a path the controller names when it starts the broker, so a lead cannot grant its own push.
* ''The broker runs'' from a copy outside the lane and with a launcher outside the lane; a lead's edit to the lane's own copy never reaches the running broker.
* ''A crew cap'' may ride the broker: it refuses a `launch` while a seat of the same kind stays live, naming the cap, so a lead adapts by reading the answer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-spend-guard`>>
!! The spend guard ~ true usage, read where each tool writes it

* ''True usage'' reads from each Codex rollout's `token_count` events (`total_token_usage`: input, cached input, output, reasoning) and from each rollout's `rate_limits.primary.used_percent`. A seat's printed "tokens used" line understates the draw by orders of magnitude, so no tally reads it.
* ''Cached input counts.'' Caching lowers the price of a token, never its weight against a rate limit or a plan pool.
* ''The floors'': the Codex pool keeps 20% for a build, research or lead seat and 0% for a read seat; the launcher refuses at or under the floor.
* ''The used-percent lines'': a lead step, a build seat and a research seat each run until 20% of the weekly pool remains, so each line stands at 80% used and meets the 20% floor, which refuses first; a read seat stays at its own floor; each refusal names the reading. The operator set the line 2026-11-07: "Set the codex pool limit down to 20% (its really a soft limit)." Soft: it bounds a fresh launch, never a running seat (the per-seat ceiling below bounds that), and it stands for the operator to move again.
* ''The per-seat ceiling'': the launcher starts a detached supervisor that is the seat's parent: it spawns codex itself, so the child pid cannot be reused before the supervisor reaps it, and it signals only its own child. It tails the seat's own rollout (matched by the seat's own marker, which rides the first line of its prompt: the first user text in its rollout whose first line opens with `SEAT-MARKER ` decides, so the AGENTS.md project doc and environment context Codex records as user messages ahead of the brief decide nothing, an echo in tool output never counts, and a rollout whose turn answers or ends with no seat prompt ahead of it reads as another session; a seat with no NEW token event for 200 ticks (ten minutes; a re-read of the last total does not count; `--blind-ticks N` moves the limit and the default stays 200) is stopped, and any error while watching stops it too, failing closed. The stop writes ceiling.md FIRST, then runs the ladder (SIGTERM to the group, grace, SIGKILL to the group, a wait for the exit event); a signal that throws never reads as stopped, and a watch that ends with the child alive makes the supervisor SIGKILL its own child. The relay's go and state live under the SAME fixed broker root, `<root>/go/<lane-id>/push-go` and `<root>/iso/<lane-id>`, where the lane id is the lane's base name plus a digest of its REAL path (two lanes sharing a base name never share a go or a state dir). The controller's `tools/push-go.mjs` writes a four-line go (lane id, branch, head, push destination) and the relay refuses a go whose lane or destination differs from the one it holds. No caller names a path (a go or state dir anywhere under `~/.lares-scratch`, another lane included, refuses); a made worktree stages in `.broker-worktrees/<lane-id>` under scratch (verified like the root: real directories, ours, exactly 0700), no granted root (worktree, seat dir, lane root) may equal, contain or sit inside that staging tree except the exact made worktree a build seat was handed, and a build or lead worktree outside scratch refuses. A launched seat runs in a BROKER-OWNED seat directory under that ONE fixed broker root that no caller names: `~/.local/state/lares-broker` with `seats`, `go` and `iso` inside it (moved only by the `LARES_BROKER_ROOT` environment the controller sets), outside `~/.lares-scratch` entirely, made 0700, verified by lstat on every component from the home down (real directories, ours, no group- or world-writable ancestor, the root exactly 0700), with a size bound (`LARES_BROKER_MAX_GB`, ten by default) that refuses a launch by name, a fresh `mkdtemp` seat dir per launch, and the seat dir removed after the hand-back or a refusal. A dry run creates nothing. Codex runs inside a PID namespace (`bwrap --unshare-pid --die-with-parent`, measured to nest with Codex's own sandbox on this host), so when the namespace's init dies every descendant dies, setsid or not; a live seat refuses by name where that wall cannot stand. Every supervision exit path (a throw, a clean exit, an unstopped group, a size stop) ends in one `finally` that removes the broker seat dir, or keeps it and names it in the marker while its group still lives, and a running seat's seat dir has a live byte bound (`LARES_SEAT_MAX_BYTES`, two GiB by default; over it the seat stops with verdict `size-ceiling`). The namespace contains every descendant but does not reserve the host process-group id after the child is reaped; that pid-reuse edge stays accepted. The launch message names a completion marker `done-<uuid>` the supervisor writes LAST (it carries the verdict, the exit code, the names copied and any hand-back error, and it stands even when the supervision itself fails; the wait also watches the supervisor's pid), a containment command that fails before Codex runs reads `setup-failed`, and every group signal rides one guard, `killGroup`, that refuses any pgid that is not an integer above 1 (a pid-1 test fake once reached `kill(-1)` and killed every process the operator owns; signal-spawning suites run only inside a bwrap PID namespace, and a witness reads any `process.kill` outside the guard as red), and a reused lane seat dir moves its old final.md, ceiling.md, out.md and done-* into `prev-<n>/` first. Under that root: Codex's `-o final.md`, `TMPDIR`, run.log and ceiling.md all land there, where no lead can write, and the lead names a lane seat dir the broker never runs a writer against. After the seat's process group is gone, the supervisor copies the seat's regular top-level files (each capped at 8 MiB, at most 64, never symlinks or directories) into the lane's seat dir through a held handle (an old entry unlinked, then O_NOFOLLOW|O_EXCL), so a lane dir swapped mid-run redirects nothing. Every launcher write into the lane's seat dir (the brief, attempts.log, the snapshot, the marker, the prompt) goes through one directory handle opened and verified under the lane, with O_NOFOLLOW and a single-link check; lead steps hold the lane root the same way (`steps/`, the claim and `state.json` all through it); the supervisor inherits both handles as fds. A witness test reads any pathname fs write in `launch-codex.mjs` or `lane-state.mjs` as red, and an access witness reads ANY fs call on a non-held path in the four broker files (`commit-relay.mjs`, `seat-broker.mjs`, `seat-walls.mjs`, `tree-export.mjs`) as red unless it goes through a held handle or carries the broker-owned mark (the controller's own arguments, the broker root, the staging tree, the home's credential list). The staging parent's verifier also refuses a group- or world-writable scratch dir or home. When a child exits, the supervisor SIGKILLs its whole process group at once (ESRCH is fine) and confirms the group answers ESRCH before a stop reads as stopped) and sums `total_token_usage.total_tokens`; at the ceiling (15M for a lead step, 8M for build, read and research; `--ceiling N` overrides) it sends SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, and writes `ceiling.md` in the seat directory naming the reading. The watchdog never reads `run.log`. A Copilot run reads its spend from its log's `Requests N Premium` line, and a log without one reads UNMETERED, never zero.
* ''Compaction'': every seat launches with `-c model_auto_compact_token_limit=120000` and `-c tool_output_token_limit=8000`, launcher overrides only (`~/.codex/config.toml` stays the operator's). `--canary` (read mode) writes a brief that makes about 100 small tool calls in one turn and runs under a 50000 limit; `--check-canary --seat-dir D` reads that seat's rollout and prints `COMPACTION-FIRED` (exit 0) or `COMPACTION-NOT-FIRED` (exit 1) with the event, call and token counts; the launcher snapshots the existing rollouts before it spawns the seat. Measured 2026-11-04 (Luna, low effort): compaction fired inside `codex exec` (1 event, about 100 calls, 2.9M total tokens).
* ''Reasoning effort'' moves little of the draw; context length moves most of it. Effort follows the step's difficulty, never the budget.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-swarm-controls`>>
!! The swarm's controls ~ signed tiddlers in the daemon wiki, at standing

The swarm's live controls rest in the live daemon wiki as signed records, verified when read. The operator ruled it at standing, built before the founding (2026-11-04): capability, not account; identity-as-relation; no key on a surface and no key file a seat can read. The flat go file and the relay's in-memory HMAC key retire as the later slices land.

| Control | Signed by, as relation | Writer |
| the house cap (a line on the face-private board) | the operator's face, by hand | the hand only (a hand-only verb, surface origin) |
| a lane's share (a line on the face-private board) | the face by hand, or the controller under a cap the hand granted | hand / controller |
| `$:/lares/swarm/go/<branch>` | the face by hand, or the controller under a granted cap | binds branch and head; spent on the attempt |
| `$:/lares/swarm/lane/<lane>/state` | the lead, under its own seat name | the lead |
| `$:/lares/swarm/roster/<seat>` | the VESSEL (the daemon's device to face relation), as witness | the watchdog reports; the daemon signs; never the measured lead |
| `$:/lares/swarm/receipt/<tree>` | the VESSEL, same | the broker asks after the walled checks pass; the daemon signs |

A lead may add note rows beside its roster rows under its own seat name; it never signs spend. The house cap and the lane shares rest on the face-private promote board, never as daemon-wiki titles: their `$:/lares/swarm/house-cap` and `lane/<lane>/share` titles name the family, and no record rests there. A lead's trust binds its seat, its lane and its key together, so a lead signs its own lane's state alone.

* ''The model stands'' (`swarm-tiddlers`, `swarm-board`). Every record signs under the `swarm-act` domain, its own name, so no swarm record verifies as a promote or kahua act. A read checks shape, domain, face group, signature, the title the body belongs at and the signer's role; any failure reads as absent, so a read fails closed. The house cap and the lane shares ride the face-private promote board as a sibling record family (they cross devices: the whole swarm answers to one cap); each line folds by causal heads and concurrent heads take the minimum, so a fork only tightens the swarm. A line that cites a parent it lacks, or holds no head, reads zero and UNAVAILABLE by its reason (`parent-missing`, `no-head`): `swarm-show` names it, and a write onto it refuses `swarm-line-unavailable` unless it is a hand's explicit `supersede`, which cites the line's dangling heads and starts the line again. A torn record enters the refused, never silence. The board's integrity rests on who can write the face's board doc and the daemon doc: verify-on-read refuses a forged record, but a writer of those docs can still drop records or tear them, which reads as an unavailable line. Shares never sum past the cap: the write refuses it, and a board whose shares sum past the cap folds every share to zero. A go binds a branch and a head, carries a nonce, and is spent once: the vessel claims one spend record per go atomically, so racing spenders land one. Go, lane state, roster and receipts rest in the daemon document, device-local by its anchor.
* ''Off disk.'' The `$:/lares/swarm/` prefix reads as control plane (`isControlPlaneUri`) and never projects (`neverProjects`), under every spelling that starts with it or resolves beneath it, a dot segment that folds out of it included; each record also carries `disk-projection: "no"`. A name refuses before it can spell such an escape: no swarm name, decoded or not, holds an empty, `.` or `..` segment, a one-segment name (lane, seat, tree, spend) holds no `/`, and a go's branch keeps git's ref-name rules (`git check-ref-format`).
* ''The verbs.'' `swarm-show` reads (open to a seat); `swarm-cap-set`, `swarm-share-set` and `swarm-go-issue` are hand-only (`swarm-needs-hand`), at the socket and on the daemon island. They sign through `SwarmSignerPort`, wired to the ward's signer (#/the-ward): each signing row opens the face root fresh under the route typed for it, so a script holding unlocked custody signs none. The `who` check stays advisory for `show` alone.
* ''Before the wiki stands'', every swarm act refuses by name: "no standing daemon: swarm controls need the founded wiki". There is no flat-file fallback.
* ''Still to land'': the co-sign of a second device for the house cap and the push go (S3); the capability channel and the unix-socket probe (S4); the vessel-signed roster and receipt, the broker swap and the retirement of the flat go and the HMAC key (S5). The go's spend and the vessel's claim are modelled (`spendGo`, `trust.vessel`) and stay unwired until S5. A two-device witness that swarm tiddlers stay device-local, a live probe of the store-door rows against a founded face, and a probe of whether a seat's `meme-put` or a synced tiddler can reach the daemon doc's `$:/lares/swarm/` titles stay owed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-ward`>>
!! The ward ~ the face root opens fresh for one hand act, never from unlocked custody

The operator ruled it (2026-11-04): users keep full caps over their own wikis, while the daemon wiki and the kahua stage are bound by the ward, their startup actions included. Never a passkey or WebAuthn: that binds the house to a web2.5 registry.

* ''The key.'' Each face holds a WARD key in its own slot tree (`faces/<fcid>/ward-slots.bin`, a floor row), bound over the face's human routes (a passphrase, a paper pin) and never a passkey (`ward-passkey-refused`, at bind, in a hand-edited tree, and as a credential). The face root seed rests `ward-cold`: sealed under the ward key in the ward's own frame (`lares-ward-seal`, domain `custody-carrier-ward`), never under the face key, so the census faults an FCK-framed root where a warded one belongs.
* ''One act, one unlock.'' No door of unlocked custody reads, writes, moves or removes the root (`ward-needs-fresh-unlock`). A ward act opens the ward key fresh from the route typed for it, through the minted KDF port, spends the face's own unlock attempts on a miss, signs, and zeroes the key; the key never enters the faces the holder holds open, and nothing caches a route or a key between acts.
* ''Where the route comes from.'' The CLI reads it from a TTY alone (`ward-needs-tty` refuses an environment variable and a piped stdin). It never rides a store verb's arguments (those hash into a receipt): it rides one custody frame bound to the act it names (its verb, its face and a digest of its arguments), and comes back as a one-use ref the verb carries; a ref spent on any other act refuses and burns. No module holds a route between acts: the unlock hands its route to the composition it stands (its boot dial alone), and a take to the re-present it asks for, each as an argument. A sealed act carries it in its own `ward` argument. A founding rides the route its face is born under. The node panel carries none (its verbs ride the daemon document), so its bound acts refuse by name toward the terminal. A browser face holds its own ward the same way (`ward/h<N>/slots` beside its `ward-cold` root at `ward/h<N>/persona-root`, in the ward's frame; the keystore's tree key wraps neither, and every custody row door refuses the root `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`): it signs in a per-act ward worker, fresh from the passphrase the ceremony document's bytes carry for that act (never a page dialog; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/browser-vessel-lift`>>), one signature per route, three misses per vessel thread; an act whose mesh code derives from the seed (the founding edge, a handle publish, burn or attest) opens the ward for that one act in the vessel worker's own context. A browser founding binds its face's ward over the route that founding's custody asked for, or asks the ceremony to bind one, typed twice alike; a mint binds the new face's ward over the route typed twice alike for it. The walk's possession check reads the sealed roster and the ward's frame, so an unattended walk asks for nothing. No file answers for a route or a new face's route: a test harness seats its own explicitly (`tools/test-ward-route.mjs`, in its CLI processes alone).
* ''The bound acts.'' Every act that reads the root: a daemon or kahua `promote-commit` (its ledger row carries `wardSig` over (wiki, digest, seq), and boot voids a bound row without one, however the row reached the ledger), `kapae-lower`, the swarm's house cap, lane shares and push go, and every act that changes who belongs to the face or what it trusts: admit, fleet-join, recovery contacts, the handles, nexus publish, the lease, the intent root, a holder's edge kāpae, the seal witness, backup and restore, the founder's certificate at a new scope. `kapae-raise` only adds a brake, so it fails safe and signs as the device's own atom.
* ''A face founded before the ward'' refuses `ward-absent` until `lares vault rewrap --face N` binds its ward and moves its root under it (the tree first, so a torn rewrap resumes).

Residuals, named: the ward is only as strong as the passphrase, offline; a seed-deriving browser act (a handle publish, burn or attest; the founding edge) holds the opened seed on the vessel worker's thread for its act (the vessel runs in its own worker, never the page: browser-vessel-lift), zeroing the seed bytes in the act's finally but leaving the hex strings they derive from, which it cannot zero; the founding reuses the founder face's first-bind route as the ward route, and that route lives as a closure value in the vessel worker until its one sign; a browser face's ward and its roster still name the face by its handle (`ward/h0/…`, the roster's index), the one face a founding wears, where every other face row names its PersonaGroup; the browser keeps no rewrap verb, so a face founded before its ward refuses (its roster no longer reads) until it founds again; the founder certificate at a new scope issues inside the face-unlock act, under the route the person just typed; the kahua lift, withdraw and carry sign with the root and so ride the ward, though the ruling names every lift free (freeing them needs a device-atom lift in the kahua reader, the operator's call); a racer that reads a ward ref off the daemon document can spend it only on the identical act the operator asked for (binding it to the socket waits on S4's channel); a dropped bound promotion leaves a tombstone in the same device-local ledger row the face's custody writes, so ANY plaintext edit by a writer holding unlocked custody can strip that tombstone and replay the dropped row, not only a whole-row rollback; the cure (a floor that writer cannot forge: a board-anchored drop shadow, or a ward-signed ledger head) stands owed after standing as the road's `L23`, and lands with no re-genesis since the ledger is device-local; the co-sign of a second device (S3) gates the lower, the house cap and the push go (`S3b`, #/the-swarm-slices).

