!! Owed ~ the rows this design still lacks

# A deep read and debug pass over the whole harness once the wiki server stands (the Terra rounds continue there; the nine rounds so far found their classes by placement and authority, and the residuals named below stand open until then: the host process-group id after a reap, the sampled size ceiling, the ptrace and seccomp assumptions around the in-memory receipt key).
# ''The harness lane's calls'' (2026-11-07, talked out against the agentic-harness collision report and the Gaia 2026 and Elyncia 5492 stories; each one overrulable):
## Lane state gains a `generation` that `writeState` checks before it writes, so a stale lead never overwrites a newer step. No time lease: a clock deadline breaks No global now.
## The fresh lead per step holds. A measure row compares fresh and resumed steps by cached and uncached tokens (`rolloutTokens`), not raw totals, before anything changes.
## A deterministic controller (readiness, receipts, land) waits until the Lararium stands; meanwhile each row moves one controller judgment into a tool, as the claim moved "is the broker late".
## A broker door for local e2e stays parked. The no-local-e2e ruling guards wall-clock time and allows one named e2e test, so a one-test door would not overturn it; it reopens when a lead step names "waiting on an e2e only the controller can run" as its stop, and then lands as one named-test door under `DOOR_RUN_MAX_MS` with fake keys and its own network namespace.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-swarm-slices`>>
!! Row 7's slices ~ order, state and the design of S3 to S6

The wiki-daemon key cap stands in six slices, each red-first with its control. The operator ruled it at standing (2026-11-04) and ruled the ward's reach (2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>). The founding waits on `S1` to `S3`, `S2b`, `S3-pre-b` and `S3b`; `S4` to `S6` follow the standing.

|! Slice |! Holds |! State, 2026-11-06 |
| `S1` | the swarm tiddler model and the hand-only verbs | landed with `S2` (PR #167, `a9f461bf4`) |
| `S2` | the ward: a `ward-slots.bin` tree under each face, a fresh unlock per act, passkey refused | landed (PR #167, `a9f461bf4`): the marker cure, the ward-dial-in-flight refusal by name, the witness slot audit |
| `S2b` | the browser root under the ward | landed (PR #169, `6f5bf8c56`): a per-face ward tree in the keystore, the root `ward-cold` beside it, every custody row door refusing it by name, a one-use signer per route, passkey refused, founding and mint born under the ward, the walk's possession check seedless |
| `S3a` | the co-sign judge, the self-answer cure, `ask --detail`, the blinded per-face vessel tag over a random per-face tag secret (a D row, vessel-local, derived from no seed) | landed (PR #168, `f2b786887`) |
| `S3-pre-b` | the founding and join wiring of the vessel tag, default `required`: the founding mints the founder face's tag secret under its ask and its settle moves it onto the group's row; the join request mints the joinee face's | landed (PR #168, `f2b786887`); founding-critical |
| `S3b` | the gate hook that calls the judge, the readers that hold each act to its outcome, and the co-signer's own ward | landed (PR #171, `ffe05bc79`): the kāpae lower (node store row, node panel, browser), the house cap and the push go run through it; the board's fold, the cap's fold and the go's reader read each act only beside its spent co-sign, frozen at its outcome; a go names the go it follows and the branch's go ends its longest co-signed chain; a replay after a crash runs nothing; the co-signer's approve opens its own ward and carries that ward's per-face key's signature over (the answer, the ask's digest, the frontier it read), the key riding the device's admission; the outcome cites the answer's frontier; the browser answers through its own ward; and the answer door refuses an ask it cannot see or one already spent |
| `S4` | the capability channel and the AF_UNIX probe | designed below |
| `S5` | vessel-signed roster and receipt, the broker swap | designed below |
| `S6` | canon | designed below |

!!! The S3 cures the reads found

An Opus co-sign reader read LAND-AFTER-CURES and found the pinned-audience hole; Terra read HOLD, its ranks rolled into C2 to C4 and the owed residues; a Terra re-read of the cures read LAND-AFTER-CURES and found R1 and R2.
* ''C1.'' The co-sign verbs need audience `asks`.
* ''C2.'' The plane counts only an ask its issuer signed, answered by a distinct standing device.
* ''C3.'' The root signs only after the tag checks pass.
* ''C4.'' A replay appends nothing.
* ''R1.'' A replay serves the seated edge only when its digest matches; otherwise it refuses `replay-edge-lapsed`.
* ''R2.'' A stale table fails closed, by ward verb name.
* ''Owed residues'' rest as road rows in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/stride-0-hearth`>> (`L24` to `L27`).

!!! Live holes the scouts found

Each cure rides the slice named.
* `S3`: the intent plane approves a SELF-answer. Any enrolled device signs an answer, and the read checks only that the answer's executor equals the acting atom, so the asker approves its own ask.
* `S4`: `tools/launch-codex.mjs` hands the supervisor fds 3 and 4 without close-on-exec, and the bwrap spawn probably carries them into bwrap and Codex. Treat it as red until a witness measures that the seat sees only fds 0 to 2.
* A same-uid process can open `session:open` with hand `cli` and wear the unlocked face (the connector defaults the hand to `cli`, and `cli` maps to the `verb` profile, which reaches every hand-only act, since the socket refuses hand-only acts for the `seat` profile alone). The ward closes that for bound acts. The lift posture for user wikis stays advisory by ruling.
* Node has no `socketpair(2)` and cannot pass an fd to an unrelated live process.
* `S3` (road `L24`), the renewal writer: a joinee seated with its tag that lost its answer and met a lease roll refused `replay-edge-lapsed` on its retry and had no way to boot. The join answer now reads the seated edge off the fold (the latest verified `device-edge` act for that device) and, where the lease rolled past it, the root appends one `device-edge` act for the seated admission at the current epoch and serves that edge; the answer serves what the log holds, and a request whose cached answer the log no longer seats still refuses `replay-edge-lapsed`. It never appends a second admission and never moves the tag, and a device the fleet set aside refuses `DeviceSetAside` ahead of it. Vector: `device-admit-edge-renewal.test.ts`, on the real KEL, fold, lease slot and payload producer. A roll itself still strands every booted device, the rolling vessel's own among them, at its next boot: each one's sealed anchor pins the edge digest it landed, and only founding, admission and recovery move that pin. That law stands declared in the red register (`A LEASE ROLL LEAVES EVERY SEATED DEVICE ABLE TO BOOT`) and waits on the operator's ruling over who moves the pin at a roll.
* `S3` (road `L24`), the lease guard (house ruling 2026-11-05; the operator may overrule it): until the renewal at the roll stands, `lares persona lease` refuses `lease-roll-strands-devices` (`LeaseRollStrandsDevices`) before it signs or writes, whenever this vessel's own edge binds the epoch the roll would leave or the face's `@persona` log seats another device's edge; the log carries an edge's digest and never its bound epoch, so every other seated edge counts. The refusal names the count and this owed row, and the CLI prints it. The ruling it records: a pin that moves to a renewed edge the root signed, that the log holds as the atom's latest `device-edge` act at a newer epoch, reads as a device-local cache update, not an identity change; the renewal at the roll builds after standing under that reading. Vector: `persona-lease-verb.test.ts`, on the production verb, the real persona KEL and a real log.
* `S3`, the floor-door refusal: the floor key's door (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`) refuses every `vessel-face-tag*` name beside the sovereign gate bundle's, so no later call derives a vessel-wide tag secret that would link this vessel's faces; the registry keeps both tag names for the per-face key. Vector: `gate-bundle-custody.test.ts`; the floor gate bundle still derives. A founding killed between the tag's mint under its ask and its settle onto the group's row resumes on the same tag and settles one group row (`founding-seed-crash.test.ts`, a pin: it greened with no cure).

!!! S3 ~ the co-sign

* A pure judge, `judgeCosign({view, intentId, requester, projection, standing, digest})` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign.ts`. The device count reads the projection's signed admission fold: zero or one device answers `none` (the fresh unlock alone), two or more answer `cosign`.
* A co-sign approves only when the answer carries a signature by an enrolled device that differs from the requester and whose standing reads `stands`; the ask's signer equals the requester; the ask's detail equals the digest of (act, face, wiki, head cid); the answer stands uncontested; and the intent stands unspent. Set-aside devices count toward the total and never co-sign. The co-signing device fresh-unlocks too: its approve of a ward act signs only inside a fresh unlock of its own ward for the face (`signIntentAnswer` in `lararium-node/src/commands/intent.ts`), opened from its own route for that act and zeroed after, a passkey never opening it. A joinee holds no face root, yet its ward stands from its join: the join request founds the face's custody, and that founding binds the ward beside it. The store door runs `intent-answer` first with no route and asks one only where the signer names `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`; a deny, and an answer to any other ask, ask none. The answer door signs no answer to an ask this vessel cannot see (`ward-ask-unseen`: nothing tells it whether the ask names a ward act) and none to a ward act whose outcome already spent it (`ward-ask-spent`).
* THE WARD ON THE WIRE (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). Each device's admission into a face (the founding admission and the fleet-join request alike) carries its per-face ward key (`wardKey`) under the tag key's signature over (group, atom, ward key); beside it rides `wardPop`, that ward key's own signature over (group, atom, ward key) under `ward-key-pop` (`wardKeyPopImage`, `wardKeyPopHolds`), so the device proves it holds the key it names. The fold refuses `ward-key-absent`, and `ward-key-unproven` where the pop fails or stands absent, and keeps each seated device's key (`wardKeys`); a join request with none refuses `request-ward-key-absent`, one whose pop fails `request-ward-key-unproven`, and the hearth's admit refuses `ward-key-unproven` before its root signs. The pop binds the atom, which each face mints fresh per device, so it needs no nonce, and the fold reads it off the admission alone. A node's tag door signs the pop inside one fresh unlock of the face's ward from the act's route (the founding's own, or the one the joinee's face custody is born under, else the person at the terminal, else `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`); a browser's door signs it inside the per-act ward worker from the route typed for that act, the mint's own act where that ask mints the key and a fresh one where the key stood (a door with no route refusing `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`, a passkey `ward-passkey-refused`). A node derives the ward signing seed from the face's own ward key (HKDF under `ward-sign-seed`; the ward key mints at random per face at the founding or the join), writes its public half to the face's `face-ward-key` D row as the ward founds or rewraps (THE REWRAP KEEPS THE KEY: a rewrap over a standing ward keeps its key, and one from an absent ward over a face whose key row stands refuses `ward-key-would-rotate`, since that ward founded the key the admission names and its sealed rows stay sealed under the missing tree), and signs through `WardSeat.wardSign` inside the fresh unlock; a browser's ward key rests under a ward of its own, apart from the keystore's tree and from the face root's ward: its tree at `ward/sign/<group>/slots`, its seed sealed in the ward's frame at `ward/sign/<group>/seed`, its public half in plain bytes at `ward/sign/<group>/public-key`, and its logical name `ward-sign/<group>` refused by every ordinary custody door by name (`ward-needs-fresh-unlock`). The mint asks its route twice, `bind` then `retype` (`browserWardBindRoute`), and binds only where both match: a mismatch refuses `ward-route-mismatch` and binds nothing, since a ward bound over a typo opens for nobody; every ask over a standing ward names `open`, and the dialog's words follow the kind (`wardAskWords`: a bind binds a passphrase for this face's ward, an open opens it for one act). Each tag ask sweeps the face's sealed ward-key row from before the key moved under its own ward (`sealed/ward-sign/<group>`), and only the test-only entry (`@lararium/browser/testing`) seats the ward worker's spawn: no door takes a caller's. It mints inside one worker act (`runWardKeyMint`: a fresh ward binds over the typed route and proves it reopens, a random seed seals under it and proves it reopens, the pop signs once, and all of it zeroes), the page landing the three rows in one transaction only where none stands; every later pop and every approve signs inside a fresh worker act (`browserWardKeySign`), and the tag door names the key off the plain row with no seed opened. A founding hands the mint its own ward route; a join request, the page's ward dialog. No seed and no vessel-wide key derives either, so two faces of one vessel present ward keys no reader links. An approve carries `frontier` (the fleet frontier head the answerer read) and `wardSig`, that key's signature over (the answer, the ask's digest, the frontier) under `ward-answer` (`wardAnswerImage`, `buildWardedAnswer`). The judge counts an approve only where `wardSig` verifies under the answerer's admitted ward key (else `ward-answer-unwarded`), the gate's outcome cites the frontier the answer names, and the witness reads an outcome only where it cites that very frontier beside a holding ward signature. The node's answer door refuses an approve with no known frontier (`ward-cosign-frontier-unknown`); the browser's answer door (`browserWardAnswer`, the vessel's `ward-answer` verb) approves through its ward, a passkey route refusing `ward-passkey-refused`, and a deny opens no ward on either. The browser's ward act opens the ward key's own ward, never the keystore's tree, and rides `B1`'s ceremony once it stands. An approval binds one ask's digest, and the frozen spend refuses its replay; a ward key never rotates silently: a rewrap keeps it, and a device whose ward goes missing re-joins as a new vessel.
* `ward-kapae-lower`, `ward-house-cap` and `ward-push-go` register as single-effect face intent verbs with audience `asks`. The outcome slot spends the intent.
* The gate (an `S2` ward act) calls the judge. When a co-sign applies, it writes the ask and refuses `ward-awaiting-cosign: <ask cid>`: no clock, no timeout, no fallback. An offline second device refuses for good until it answers.
* The gate stands as `wardCosignGate` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign-gate.ts`, inside the doors themselves: `promoteBoardDoor`'s lower and `swarmCommand`'s cap and go each read the face's intent plane through a `cosign` port the vessel composes at the act (`nodeWardCosignPort` on a node, `browserWardCosignPort` in a browser). It reads through `judgeCosign` alone and never through `mayAct`. The act's intent takes the act's digest as its nonce, so one act at one head names one intent: the first reach issues it with its issuer-signed ask and refuses, a retry names the same ask and writes nothing, an approved act runs and then signs the outcome that spends it, and a replay refuses `ward-cosign-spent`. Every other judge refusal refuses `ward-cosign-<reason>`. A lower names the edge's standing heads and refuses `kapae-heads-moved` where they move under the answer; a cap names its value and the line it extends. The lane share rides the fresh unlock alone. Vectors: `ward-cosign-table.ts`, one table that the mesh, node and browser (chromium) rows run over the real doors and a real intent document, with a witness that the gate calls the judge and no `mayAct`.
* The readers hold every act to its outcome, never the door alone. A witness over the face's intent plane (`wardActWitness` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign.ts`) answers each reader: the promote board's fold reads a lower, the swarm board's fold a cap and `readGo` a go only where the face's signed admission fold holds zero or one device, or beside a spent co-sign. The spend stands FROZEN at its outcome: the gate's outcome cites the answer it spent and the fleet frontier head it read, and the witness judges that cited answer at that frontier (an answer to the issuer's own ask naming the act's digest, an approve naming the issuer as executor, from another device of the face, and both that device and the issuer standing at the cited frontier: no raise it counts sets either aside). What lands after the outcome against the co-signer, a second approve, a deny, a raise the next frontier counts, never changes what stands; the judge reads any outcome the issuer signed as spent. THE ISSUER READS NOW, REMOVAL WINS (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults; from the edge-frontier ruling "a raise shadows its target at once for every reader that sees it"): a reader that sees a device set aside at its own fleet frontier refuses that device's outcomes and its certificate alike (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-offline-root`>>); the witness reads the issuer at the reader's current standing, so an outcome whose issuer this reader reads aside refuses `ward-issuer-aside` and one it reads `unknown` refuses `ward-issuer-unknown`, door-made or signed outside the gate citing an approve from before the set-aside, and a refused cap at the tip of the cap line BRAKES it: the cap reads the tightest of every cap the line holds, the refused among them, never an older looser one the refusal uncovers (it fails toward the brake, as a lower and a go do); a hand's supersede citing the refused tip roots the line again, and `show` names the line `braked`. The witness names its refusal (`wardActRefusal`), and the promote board, the swarm board and the go chain carry the name. The port reads the frontier head (`frontier()`, null where it does not read known) and a device's standing at one link of the fleet's chain (`stoodAt`, over `fleetFrontierLinks` and `fleetStoodAt` in `fleet-frontier.ts`), on node and browser alike, and the links read on while the head waits on an uncounted raise. An act the root signed outside the door, or an approved intent with no outcome, refuses `ward-act-uncosigned`; a fold that cannot be read stands no lower, so the brake holds. Every vessel composition hands its readers the witness over the same port its doors hand the gate; the always-accepting check the doors use for their own replay search (`ward-door-decided.ts`) stays out of the package index, and the node's custody fixture seam (`seatFaceAutoFounder`) rides the test-only entry `@lararium/node/testing`.
* A go names the go it follows at its branch (`after`: that go's cid as `show` reads it, null for the branch's first), never a clock or a counter: a fresh go for one head names a new intent and asks afresh, and a replay of an approved go names the spent one and refuses `ward-cosign-spent`. Each go rests at its own row (`go/<branch>/<go cid>`), and the branch's standing go ends the longest co-signed `after` chain from its first go (`readGoChain`): an older go written again ends a shorter chain and never stands, a go chained onto an orphan stands on no chain, and two tips read as a fork (`forked`) where neither stands until a fresh go names one. At the door a go naming an `after` a co-signed go already follows refuses `swarm-go-forked`, and one naming no tip refuses `swarm-go-after-moved`, both before the gate asks. `show` names `after` only from the standing tip, and a fork's tips as `forks`.
* The effect lands first and its outcome after it, for each act: an outcome written first would strand the act, since the same words (the same heads, the same cap line, the same `after`) name a spent intent with nothing landed. The readers read the act only beside its outcome, so a crash between the two leaves it unread; each effect finds its own earlier run by the act's digest, so the replay signs nothing, lands the owed outcome and refuses `ward-act-already-run`.
* Residuals, named: the browser page passes the route typed for a ward act over the worker's port, so a script running in the page when the person types it reads it; the web page's fleet-join request rides the page's own ward dialog until `B1`'s ceremony (slice 3) replaces it, and that ceremony carries the same two kinds: a bind asked twice alike, an open asked once. A device whose ward tree goes missing (a node) or whose ward key rows go missing (a browser) holds no key its admission names, so its approves stop verifying until the face re-founds or the device is admitted again under a fresh face atom; no rotation act stands, since the root rows that would sign one stay sealed under the missing ward. An act signed while the face held one device reads refused once a second device stands (it fails toward the brake: the hand lowers or sets it again under the co-sign). The co-signer's answer carries the frontier it read, so an answer signed before a raise that set its signer aside still cites the link it read; the raise binds what follows it, never an answer already signed. The issuer reads at each reader's current standing, so a reader whose fleet frontier reads `unknown` stands no co-signed act of that issuer until it reads known. A go chain lives in the device-local daemon wiki, so a writer that deletes its newer rows rolls the chain back; no reader holds the chain's history past its rows. A newer act whose cited link has not reached a reader reads unread there, and on the cap line that newer cap brakes the line at its tightest until the link syncs.
* The device count counts vessels, never atoms: each admission carries the vessel's blinded tag for that face, and the fold refuses a second admission whose tag already stands. The tag rides a random tag secret the device mints per face, at the founding or at the join request, in that face's own D row (`vessel-face-tag`, `bundle: stays`); no seed derives it, so a thief holding the place seed links no two faces, and a re-join on one face presents the same tag. Reds: two faces' tags read apart from any place-seed child, the row sorts D and stays, a re-join re-presents its tag; control: another face tags apart, and the seed census reads any reach named `vesselFaceTagSecret` open.
* Reds: a ward mint whose retype differs binds nothing (`ward-route-mismatch`), at the tag door and at a founding's own ward; a cap tightened under the co-sign whose issuer the reader then reads aside reads its tightest, never the looser older cap (`swarm-board.test.ts`); the tag ask leaves no sealed ward-key row from before the move; a product caller's spawn reaches no ward door; a face name holding "/" refuses `BrowserWardRefused` (`browser-ward-retype.test.ts`); a self-answer must not approve; a digest mismatch, an aside answerer and a contested pair refuse; a replay after the outcome refuses `spent`; with two devices and a peer that never answers, a thousand retries stay `ward-awaiting-cosign`; on two devices a lower, a cap and a go the root signed outside the door read `ward-act-uncosigned`; on three devices a second approve or a deny after the outcome, and a co-signer set aside or reading `unknown` after it, leave the landed act standing; an outcome citing a frontier that counts its own issuer aside stands nothing; an answer to an ask the vessel cannot see refuses `ward-ask-unseen`, and one to a spent ward act `ward-ask-spent`; a go rollback, a fork and an orphan chain never stand, and a second go naming a followed `after` refuses `swarm-go-forked`; the product indexes export neither the always-accepting check nor the custody fixture seam; a fresh go for a gone head asks afresh; a crash between an effect and its outcome replays to `ward-act-already-run` with one effect; a co-signer's approve with no fresh unlock refuses `ward-needs-fresh-unlock`; an approve its atom alone signed, or one warded under another device's admitted key, refuses `ward-answer-unwarded` and stands no outcome; an issuer outcome citing an older frontier than its answer carries stands nothing; two faces of one vessel present one ward key (node and browser); a browser page holding its keystore unlocked reads the face's ward seed through the custody doors and signs a valid approve and pop with no ward act, a browser tag door with no route mints the ward key and signs its pop on the page, and a re-read opens the seed on the page to name its key (`browser-ward-key.test.ts`); an issuer set aside after an approve signs its outcome outside the gate citing that approve at the frontier it stood at and the reader accepts it, and an issuer set aside after a door-made outcome still stands its act (now `ward-issuer-aside`, `ward-issuer-unknown`); the browser holds no answer door, and a passkey route on it refuses `ward-passkey-refused`; a gated row that the panel answers with a thrown `ward-needs-fresh-unlock` asks its route and runs again. Controls: a second device approves, a one-device fleet lands on the unlock alone and reads an act on its own, a replay of an approved go refuses spent, an approve under the co-signer's own route lands, a standing issuer's door-made outcome stands, the ward worker signs a valid pop and approve under a fresh route while the face root's `S2b` ward rows stand byte for byte, an approved intent with no outcome reads nothing, and a straight go chain stands at its tip alone. A table-driven case runs over node and browser vessels.

!!! S4 ~ the capability channel

* The swarm verbs form their own `swarmDoor` and never join the session acts the custody socket admits, so a session of any hand (`cli`, `tab`, `seat`) that reaches for a swarm act refuses `act-rides-the-custody-door`.
* A ward act, `swarm-holder-start {kind: broker|watchdog}`, makes the daemon spawn the holder from a pinned tool path with `stdio[3]="pipe"` (libuv's socketpair) through an `sh` double fork, so the holder reparents to init: the holder stands beside the daemon, never under it. A ppid-chain witness proves the daemon pid sits in no seat's or holder's ancestry, with a direct-child spawn as its red control. Only if that witness cannot hold does a daemon-minted bearer ride the broker's inherited pipe, zeroed after use.
* The daemon identifies a holder by which end of the pair spoke, and no token rides a frame. The broker holds go-read, go-spend, receipt-sign, receipt-verify and cap-read; the watchdog holds roster-write alone. An EOF or a dead daemon reads `cap-gone`, and a restart mints fresh pairs.
* The probe measures first. A fixture server (never the daemon) takes a connect under bwrap plus Codex's sandbox with the network off and workspace write (`codex sandbox linux` or `codex debug landlock`; no model call). `tools/seat-socket-probe.expect.json` pins the measured result, and the hermetic arms assert both outcomes: a connect that succeeds without a capability gets `no-cap`, and a denied connect gets EPERM. Until the probe measures, a Codex seat counts as able to reach the socket, so the verbs ride the fd and never the face-wearing socket path.
* The fd cure closes fds 3 and up before `exec bwrap`.

!!! S5 ~ the broker swap

* A client over the inherited capability fd, `tools/swarm-door.mjs`, carries the frames: `go-read` (the daemon verifies the hand's `wardSig` and the co-sign outcome, spends once and returns branch and head), `receipt-sign` (a vessel signature in the domain `swarm-receipt`, writing `receipt/<tree>`), `receipt-verify`, `cap-read` and, for the watchdog, `roster-write`. A locked custody answers `swarm-vessel-locked`.
* The flat go file, the relay's HMAC key, its receipts map and the go lane directory retire (`push-go.mjs` goes; the isolated state stays). With no capability fd the relay refuses at start, before it makes a broker root, with "no standing daemon: swarm controls need the founded wiki", and writes nothing. The watchdog stops a seat on a capability EOF, and the ceiling still rides the launch argument.
* Reds, against a stub daemon pair end: a push whose go came only from the stub pushes (control); no record gives "no push-go"; a wrong head, a replay after the spend and a forged receipt all refuse; with no fd the relay exits non-zero and the broker root shows nothing new. A retirement witness (`tools/swarm-retired-witness.sh`) greps for `push-go`, `createHmac`, a `randomBytes(32)` key in the relay, the go file and the go lane directory, with a positive fixture. On the node side, a receipt signature verifies under the vessel's public key, and a go that was never warded answers `go-unsigned`.
* The residual: the receipt trust root names the daemon's vessel key.

!!! S6 ~ canon

This carrier gains its standing sections and rewrites the broker bullets and the trust-model line that name an in-memory key; the first owed row's in-memory-key residual retargets to the vessel-key residual; `kahua-memory-stage` closes the lift tension for the daemon and kahua wikis once the ward stands and keeps it open for user wikis; the road's `L22` row and the `F11` note follow.

